    "lem-rcbd-nested-contrast-model-and-projection": {
      "kind": "lemma",
      "risk": "medium",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-randomized-complete-block-design",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix integers $t\\ge2$ and $b\\ge1$. There are $b$ blocks, each containing the\nsame $t$ fixed experimental units, indexed within block $j$ by\n$i\\in\\{1,\\ldots,t\\}$. The $t$ treatments form a fixed set $\\mathcal T$ with\n$|\\mathcal T|=t$. A **complete block** is a block in which each treatment is\nassigned to exactly one unit. The blocks and their units are fixed before\nrandomization.\n\nLet $S_t$ be the set of permutations from the unit labels to $\\mathcal T$.\nThe **randomized complete-block design (RCBD)** uses assignment space\n$\\mathcal Z=(S_t)^b$ and the uniform probability law on this finite nonempty\nset. Thus each $z=(\\pi_1,\\ldots,\\pi_b)$ has probability $(t!)^{-b}$; the\nwithin-block treatment permutations are independent and individually uniform.\nThis is a finite randomized-treatment assignment model\n([[def-finite-randomized-treatment-assignment-model]]) whose units are the\n$tb$ cells and whose assignment is the only randomness supplied by the\ndesign. The design specifies no response model, Gaussian errors, additivity,\nor absence of interference. A pair is the special case $t=2,b=1$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-rank-nullity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $T:V\\to W$ be a linear map of vector spaces over $F$, with $V$\nfinite-dimensional. Then\n\n$$\\dim_F V=\\operatorname{nullity}T+\\operatorname{rank}T.$$\n\nEquivalently,\n\n$$\\dim_F V=\\dim_F(\\ker T)+\\dim_F(\\operatorname{im}T).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-finite-dimensional-orthogonal-decomposition",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $W$ is a subspace of a finite-dimensional real or complex inner product space $V$, then\n\n$$V=W\\oplus W^\\perp.$$\n\nThus every $v\\in V$ has unique vectors $w\\in W$ and $z\\in W^\\perp$ with $v=w+z$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "3.2",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-orthogonal-projection",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $W$ be a subspace of a finite-dimensional inner product space $V$. The orthogonal-decomposition theorem ([[thm-finite-dimensional-orthogonal-decomposition]]) gives\n\n$$V=W\\oplus W^\\perp$$\n\nand associates to every $v\\in V$ unique vectors $w\\in W$ and $z\\in W^\\perp$ with $v=w+z$. The **orthogonal projection onto $W$** is the function\n\n$$P_W:V\\longrightarrow W,\\qquad P_Wv=w.$$\n\nEquivalently, $P_Wv$ is the unique vector of $W$ such that $v-P_Wv\\in W^\\perp$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "3.2",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-dimension-of-a-linear-subspace",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $V$ be a vector space over a field $F$ ([[def-vector-space]]) that is\nfinite-dimensional with $\\dim_F V = n$ ([[def-dimension]]), and let $U$ be a\nlinear subspace of $V$ ([[def-linear-subspace]]). Then\n\n1. $U$ is finite-dimensional over $F$ and $\\dim_F U \\le n$;\n2. $\\dim_F U = n$ **if and only if** $U = V$;\n3. **Extension, with no choice principle.** Every linearly independent\n   $A_0 \\subseteq U$ is contained in a basis of $U$: there is a basis $B$ of $U$\n   with $A_0 \\subseteq B \\subseteq U$. Claim 1 is the case $A_0 = \\varnothing$.\n   Since $V$ is itself a linear subspace of $V$ ([[def-linear-subspace]]), claim 3\n   applies with $V$ in place of $U$, and hence to **any** finite-dimensional\n   vector space over $F$ in place of the pair $(V, U)$.\n\n**Nothing above uses a choice principle**, and claim 3 in particular is the\nfinite-dimensional substitute for the Zorn-based extension theorem stated earlier\non this page. In finite dimension the extension terminates on its own, because\n[[cor-independent-set-is-no-larger-than-a-finite-spanning-set]] bounds the size\nof an independent set and [[thm-well-ordering-principle]] then supplies a largest\none; no selection is made anywhere.\n\n**Finiteness is essential in claim 2.** Without it the equality case fails: the\ncompanion page exhibits a\nproper linear subspace of an infinite-dimensional space whose basis is\nequinumerous with a basis of the whole space.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "cor-dimension-of-a-direct-sum",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $F$ be a field ([[def-field]]), let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$, let $V$ be a vector\nspace over $F$ ([[def-vector-space]]) and let $U$ be a family of linear subspaces\n$U_i$ of $V$ indexed by $i < n$ ([[def-linear-subspace]],\n[[def-sum-of-linear-subspaces]]) with\n\n$$V \\;=\\; \\bigoplus_{i<n} U_i$$\n\n([[def-internal-direct-sum]]) and every $U_i$ finite-dimensional over $F$\n([[def-dimension]]). Then $V$ is finite-dimensional over $F$ and\n\n$$\\dim_F V \\;=\\; \\sum_{i<n} \\dim_F U_i ,$$\n\nthe right-hand side being the finite sum of [[def-monoid-finite-product]] read\nadditively in the commutative monoid $(\\mathbb{N}, +, 0)$\n([[def-nat-addition]], [[lem-nat-add-identity]],\n[[lem-nat-add-associative]], [[lem-nat-add-commutative]]).\n\n**The base case is a genuine case.** At $n = 0$ the direct sum of the empty\nfamily is $\\{0_V\\}$ and the empty sum of natural numbers is $0$, so the formula\nreads $\\dim_F\\{0_V\\} = 0$. **At $n = 2$** it reads\n$\\dim_F(U \\oplus W) = \\dim_F U + \\dim_F W$.\n\n**No choice principle is used.** The only inputs are\n[[thm-dimension-formula]] and [[thm-dimension-of-a-linear-subspace]], both of\nwhich are proved in finite dimension without one.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-row-space-column-space-nullspace-and-matrix-ranks",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $A\\in M_{m\\times n}(F)$. Its **row space** $\\operatorname{Row}(A)\\subseteq F^n$ is the span of its rows, its **column space** $\\operatorname{Col}(A)\\subseteq F^m$ is the span of its columns, and its **nullspace** is\n$$N(A):=\\{x\\in F^n:Ax=0\\}.$$\n\nThe **row rank** and **column rank** are\n$$\\operatorname{rrank}(A):=\\dim_F\\operatorname{Row}(A),\\qquad \\operatorname{crank}(A):=\\dim_F\\operatorname{Col}(A).$$\nThese dimensions are defined because each space is spanned by a finite list.\nStarting with the empty list, inspect the spanning vectors in order and retain a\nvector exactly when it is not in the span of those already retained. Induction\nshows that the retained list is independent and has the same span as the\noriginal finite list, so it is a finite basis. The **rank** of $A$ is its row\nrank; [[thm-row-rank-equals-column-rank]] proves that it also equals the column\nrank.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-row-rank-equals-column-rank",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every finite matrix over a field,\n$$\\operatorname{rrank}(A)=\\operatorname{crank}(A).$$\nBoth dimensions equal the number of pivots in any echelon form of $A$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Let $H=\\{a\\in\\mathbb R^t:\\mathbf1_t^Ta=0\\}$. The coordinate-sum map $\\mathbb R^t\\to\\mathbb R$ is onto, so [F2] gives $\\dim H=t-1$. By [F6], $\\dim\\operatorname{row}(C)=\\dim\\operatorname{im}C=\\operatorname{rank}C=q$. For every $a\\in\\mathbb R^t$, the vector $h=a-(\\mathbf1_t^Ta/t)\\mathbf1_t$ lies in $H$ and satisfies $Ch=Ca$, because $C\\mathbf1_t=0$. Thus $\\operatorname{im}(C|_H)=\\operatorname{im}C$ has dimension $q$, and [F2] gives $\\dim(H\\cap\\ker C)=t-1-q$. The row space of $C$ lies in $H$ and is orthogonal to $H\\cap\\ker C$. The orthogonal complement of $H\\cap\\ker C$ within $H$ has dimension $q$: both subspaces are finite-dimensional by [F4], and [F3]–[F5] subtract dimensions from $\\dim H=t-1$. Since this complement contains the $q$-dimensional row space, the equality case of [F4] identifies it with that row space.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "In $V$, the constant arrays form a one-dimensional space $G$, the arrays $(a_i)_{ij}$ for $a\\in H$ form a $(t-1)$-dimensional treatment space $T$, and the arrays $(d_j)_{ij}$ for $d\\in\\mathbb R^b$ with $\\sum_jd_j=0$ form a $(b-1)$-dimensional block space $B$. The replication maps from the two zero-sum coordinate spaces to $T$ and $B$ are linear bijections: their repeated rows or columns recover the original coordinates, so they transport bases and give the stated dimensions; the block zero-sum space has dimension $b-1$ by [F2] applied to its onto coordinate-sum map. These spaces are pairwise orthogonal: the treatment-block inner product is $\\sum_{i,j}a_id_j=(\\sum_i a_i)(\\sum_jd_j)=0$, and either zero-sum condition also makes the relevant inner product with $G$ zero. Their sum is direct: if three mutually orthogonal vectors sum to zero, taking the inner product with each one gives its squared norm zero. Thus $W_U=G\\oplus T\\oplus B$, and the finite-dimensional direct-sum formula [F5] gives $\\dim W_U=1+(t-1)+(b-1)=t+b-1$. The reduced space replaces $T$ by the replicated arrays from $H\\cap\\ker C$, whose dimension is $t-1-q$ by step 1.1; the same formula gives $\\dim W_R(C)=1+(t-1-q)+(b-1)=t+b-1-q$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "For any $y$, set $r_{ij}=y_{ij}-\\bar y_{i\\cdot}-\\bar y_{\\cdot j}+\\bar y_{\\cdot\\cdot}$. Summing $r$ over any row gives zero because $\\sum_j y_{ij}=b\\bar y_{i\\cdot}$ and $\\sum_j\\bar y_{\\cdot j}=b\\bar y_{\\cdot\\cdot}$; summing over any column likewise gives zero. Conversely, if $v$ is orthogonal to $T$, then its row-sum vector is orthogonal to every $a\\in H$, so all row sums are equal; orthogonality to $G$ makes their total zero, so every row sum is zero. The same argument with $B$ gives zero column sums. Thus $W_U^\\perp=E$. Also $(y-r)_{ij}=\\bar y_{i\\cdot}+\\bar y_{\\cdot j}-\\bar y_{\\cdot\\cdot}$ belongs to $W_U$. By [F3], this is the orthogonal decomposition of $y$ relative to $W_U$, proving the formula for $RSS_U$; the subspace $E$ is finite-dimensional by [F4], and [F3] gives $V=W_U\\oplus E$. Since the standard cell-coordinate basis makes $\\dim V=tb$, [F5] gives $\\dim E=tb-(t+b-1)=(t-1)(b-1)$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-2",
          "claim": "Put $P_C=C^T(CC^T)^{-1}C$. The matrix $CC^T$ is invertible since $C$ has full row rank. Direct multiplication gives $P_C^T=P_C$, $P_C^2=P_C$, and image $P_C=\\operatorname{row}(C)$. For any $x$ and $a\\in\\mathbb R^q$, $\\langle x-P_Cx,C^Ta\\rangle=a^TCx-a^TCC^T(CC^T)^{-1}Cx=0$, so [F3] identifies $P_C$ as the orthogonal projection onto $\\operatorname{row}(C)$. Consequently $T$ is the orthogonal direct sum of the reduced treatment space and the treatment arrays formed from $\\operatorname{row}(C)$. Therefore $W_U=W_R(C)\\oplus D_C$, where $D_C=\\{(a_i)_{ij}:a\\in\\operatorname{row}(C)\\}$.",
          "step": "3.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "The projection of $y$ onto $D_C$ is the array repeating $P_C\\bar y$ across all $b$ blocks: its residual is orthogonal to every repeated $a\\in\\operatorname{row}(C)$ because the inner product is $b\\,a^T(\\bar y-P_C\\bar y)=0$. Its squared norm is $b\\|P_C\\bar y\\|^2=b(C\\bar y)^T(CC^T)^{-1}(C\\bar y)$. In the orthogonal direct sum of step 3.2, $y-P_{W_R}y$ is this projection plus $y-P_{W_U}y$, and the two parts are orthogonal; expanding the squared norm gives the claimed residual-sum reduction.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "step 3.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
