    "thm-paired-randomization-contrast-moments": {
      "kind": "theorem",
      "risk": "medium",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-finite-randomized-treatment-assignment-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $N\\ge1$, and let $\\mathcal I=\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$ index the fixed\nexperimental units at the actual treatment-assignment level. Let\n$\\mathcal Z$ be a nonempty finite set of allowed assignments. If treatment\nis assigned to clusters, each cluster is one indexed unit; no separate\nprobability-sampling unit is present. The index $i$ identifies that unit,\nand no visit or repeated-measurement index is modeled here.\n\nA known assignment law is a function $p:\\mathcal Z\\to[0,1]$ satisfying\n$\\sum_{z\\in\\mathcal Z}p(z)=1$, so $(\\mathcal Z,p)$ is a finite probability\nspace ([[def-finite-probability-space-and-event]]); assignments of probability\nzero may remain in $\\mathcal Z$. A **potential-outcome schedule** is a function\n$\\theta:\\mathcal I\\times\\mathcal Z\\to\\mathbb R$, with\n$Y_i(z):=\\theta(i,z)$. The schedule and the units are fixed; the assignment\n$Z$ is the only randomness supplied by the design, and the observed response\nvector is $Y_\\theta(Z)=(Y_i(Z))_{i\\in\\mathcal I}$. The schedule is defined\nfor every allowed assignment, including zero-probability assignments, and\nmay depend on the full assignment vector, so no no-interference assumption is\nimplicit. The scientific statistical model contract\n[[def-scientific-statistical-model-contract]] requires the observation space,\nunits, quantity status, observation mechanism, support, conditioning\nconvention, and adequacy assumptions to be recorded; this model specifies\nthose fields as follows.\n\nTo express this as a statistical experiment, take the data space\n$\\mathcal X=\\mathcal Z\\times\\mathbb R^{\\mathcal I}$ with its full power-set\nsigma-algebra, which is a sigma-algebra by [[def-sigma-algebra]] and makes\n$\\mathcal X$ a measurable space ([[def-measurable-space]]), and parameter\nset $\\Theta=\\mathbb R^{\\mathcal I\\times\\mathcal Z}$. For each $\\theta\\in\\Theta$\nlet $P_\\theta$ be the law\n([[def-law-or-distribution-of-a-random-element]]) of the deterministic\nobservation map $O_\\theta(z)=(z,Y_\\theta(z))$ from $(\\mathcal Z,p)$. The map\nis measurable because its domain has the full power set, in the preimage\nsense of [[def-measurable-function-between-measurable-spaces]], and its law\nis a probability measure by\n[[lem-law-of-a-random-element-is-a-probability-measure]]. Thus\n$((\\mathcal X,2^{\\mathcal X}),\\Theta,(P_\\theta)_{\\theta\\in\\Theta})$ is an\nexperiment in the sense of [[def-statistical-experiment]]. The assignment\nlaw asserts no response-error model, Gaussian law, superpopulation\nrandomization, or sampling representativeness beyond the stated finite\nassignment mechanism. Its finite full-measure support set is\n$$S_\\theta:=\\{(z,Y_\\theta(z)):z\\in\\mathcal Z,\\ p(z)>0\\},$$\nsince $P_\\theta(S_\\theta)=\\sum_{z:p(z)>0}p(z)=1$. The record contains the\nassigned vector and every unit's response; missing or censored outcomes and\nrepeated visits require a separately declared observation mechanism. The\ninferential target is the assignment-invariance hypothesis for these $N$\nunits, not an estimand for a wider population.\n\nInference in this page holds $\\mathcal I$ and $\\theta$ fixed and averages\nover the declared law $p$; it does not condition on the realized assignment.\nThe status of the quantities is explicit: $\\theta$ and all\n$Y_i(z)$ are fixed, $Z$ is random, $(Z,Y_\\theta(Z))$ is observed, and\npotential responses under unassigned vectors are fixed but latent. The\nassignment set, weights, and implemented randomization protocol are\ndesign-controlled and should be checked against protocol records. Assignment\nlogs and response records are empirically assessable, but those checks do not\nprove that the declared law generated the data. Potential outcomes under\nunassigned vectors are fixed and latent. Their values are\n**substantively untestable** from the observed-data law; the\nassignment-invariance claim is a hypothesis tested by the theorem, not an\nempirical fact supplied by the design. These classifications do not make\nthe design or scientific model empirically true.\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-finite-population-average-treatment-effect-and-weak-null",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Consider the no-interference binary-treatment submodel of\n[[def-finite-randomized-treatment-assignment-model]] in which the allowed\nassignment set is $\\{0,1\\}^{\\mathcal I}$ and unit $i$'s response under\nassignment vector $z$ is $Y_i(z)=Y_i(z_i)$ for fixed real potential responses\n$Y_i(0)$ and $Y_i(1)$. For the fixed units, define the\n**finite-population average treatment effect** by\n$$\\tau_F:=\\frac1N\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal I}\\bigl(Y_i(1)-Y_i(0)\\bigr).$$\nThe **weak zero-average-effect null** is the hypothesis $\\tau_F=0$; it\nconstrains the average of the unit-level effects, not each effect separately.\nThe Fisher sharp null in this submodel, defined in\n[[def-fisher-sharp-null-and-predeclared-test-orderings]], requires\n$Y_i(1)=Y_i(0)$ for every unit. No probability distribution on the fixed\npotential responses is part of either definition.\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-randomized-complete-block-design",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix integers $t\\ge2$ and $b\\ge1$. There are $b$ blocks, each containing the\nsame $t$ fixed experimental units, indexed within block $j$ by\n$i\\in\\{1,\\ldots,t\\}$. The $t$ treatments form a fixed set $\\mathcal T$ with\n$|\\mathcal T|=t$. A **complete block** is a block in which each treatment is\nassigned to exactly one unit. The blocks and their units are fixed before\nrandomization.\n\nLet $S_t$ be the set of permutations from the unit labels to $\\mathcal T$.\nThe **randomized complete-block design (RCBD)** uses assignment space\n$\\mathcal Z=(S_t)^b$ and the uniform probability law on this finite nonempty\nset. Thus each $z=(\\pi_1,\\ldots,\\pi_b)$ has probability $(t!)^{-b}$; the\nwithin-block treatment permutations are independent and individually uniform.\nThis is a finite randomized-treatment assignment model\n([[def-finite-randomized-treatment-assignment-model]]) whose units are the\n$tb$ cells and whose assignment is the only randomness supplied by the\ndesign. The design specifies no response model, Gaussian errors, additivity,\nor absence of interference. A pair is the special case $t=2,b=1$.\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.2",
            "2.3",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-expectation-on-a-finite-probability-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **expectation** of a real random variable $X$ on a finite probability space $(\\Omega,w)$ is\n$$\\mathbb E[X]:=\\sum_{\\omega\\in\\Omega}X(\\omega)w(\\omega).$$\nFor a real constant $c$, the notation $c$ also denotes the constant random variable $\\omega\\mapsto c$.\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-moments-variance-and-covariance",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a real random variable on a probability space.\n\n- For $p>0$, the **$p$th absolute moment** of $X$ exists when $|X|^p$ is\n  integrable, and is then\n  $$\\mathbb E[|X|^p].$$\n- If $X$ is integrable, its **mean** is $\\mathbb E[X]$.\n- If $X$ is square-integrable, its **variance** is\n  $$\\operatorname{Var}(X):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])^2].$$\n\nIf $X$ and $Y$ are square-integrable real random variables on the same\nprobability space, their **covariance** is\n$$\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])(Y-\\mathbb E[Y])].$$\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.3",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-statistic-and-estimator",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathsf E=((\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A),\\Theta,(P_\\theta)_{\\theta\\in\\Theta})$\nbe an experiment ([[def-statistical-experiment]]). A **statistic** with target\nmeasurable space $(\\mathcal Y,\\mathcal B)$ is a measurable map\n$T:(\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A)\\to(\\mathcal Y,\\mathcal B)$, where measurability\nmeans that $T^{-1}(B)\\in\\mathcal A$ for every $B\\in\\mathcal B$\n([[def-measurable-function-between-measurable-spaces]]). If\n$\\psi:\\Theta\\to\\mathcal Y$ is an estimand ([[def-estimand-on-a-statistical-model]])\nand its target set $\\mathcal Y$ is equipped with $\\mathcal B$, a statistic\n$\\widehat\\psi$ with this same target space is an **estimator of $\\psi$**.\nThe value $\\widehat\\psi(x)$ is the estimate at observed data $x$. Giving\n$\\mathcal Y$ a measurable structure is an additional choice; this definition\ndoes not assert that $\\psi$ is measurable as a function of the parameter.\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-finite-randomized-treatment-assignment-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $N\\ge1$, and let $\\mathcal I=\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$ index the fixed\nexperimental units at the actual treatment-assignment level. Let\n$\\mathcal Z$ be a nonempty finite set of allowed assignments. If treatment\nis assigned to clusters, each cluster is one indexed unit; no separate\nprobability-sampling unit is present. The index $i$ identifies that unit,\nand no visit or repeated-measurement index is modeled here.\n\nA known assignment law is a function $p:\\mathcal Z\\to[0,1]$ satisfying\n$\\sum_{z\\in\\mathcal Z}p(z)=1$, so $(\\mathcal Z,p)$ is a finite probability\nspace ([[def-finite-probability-space-and-event]]); assignments of probability\nzero may remain in $\\mathcal Z$. A **potential-outcome schedule** is a function\n$\\theta:\\mathcal I\\times\\mathcal Z\\to\\mathbb R$, with\n$Y_i(z):=\\theta(i,z)$. The schedule and the units are fixed; the assignment\n$Z$ is the only randomness supplied by the design, and the observed response\nvector is $Y_\\theta(Z)=(Y_i(Z))_{i\\in\\mathcal I}$. The schedule is defined\nfor every allowed assignment, including zero-probability assignments, and\nmay depend on the full assignment vector, so no no-interference assumption is\nimplicit. The scientific statistical model contract\n[[def-scientific-statistical-model-contract]] requires the observation space,\nunits, quantity status, observation mechanism, support, conditioning\nconvention, and adequacy assumptions to be recorded; this model specifies\nthose fields as follows.\n\nTo express this as a statistical experiment, take the data space\n$\\mathcal X=\\mathcal Z\\times\\mathbb R^{\\mathcal I}$ with its full power-set\nsigma-algebra, which is a sigma-algebra by [[def-sigma-algebra]] and makes\n$\\mathcal X$ a measurable space ([[def-measurable-space]]), and parameter\nset $\\Theta=\\mathbb R^{\\mathcal I\\times\\mathcal Z}$. For each $\\theta\\in\\Theta$\nlet $P_\\theta$ be the law\n([[def-law-or-distribution-of-a-random-element]]) of the deterministic\nobservation map $O_\\theta(z)=(z,Y_\\theta(z))$ from $(\\mathcal Z,p)$. The map\nis measurable because its domain has the full power set, in the preimage\nsense of [[def-measurable-function-between-measurable-spaces]], and its law\nis a probability measure by\n[[lem-law-of-a-random-element-is-a-probability-measure]]. Thus\n$((\\mathcal X,2^{\\mathcal X}),\\Theta,(P_\\theta)_{\\theta\\in\\Theta})$ is an\nexperiment in the sense of [[def-statistical-experiment]]. The assignment\nlaw asserts no response-error model, Gaussian law, superpopulation\nrandomization, or sampling representativeness beyond the stated finite\nassignment mechanism. Its finite full-measure support set is\n$$S_\\theta:=\\{(z,Y_\\theta(z)):z\\in\\mathcal Z,\\ p(z)>0\\},$$\nsince $P_\\theta(S_\\theta)=\\sum_{z:p(z)>0}p(z)=1$. The record contains the\nassigned vector and every unit's response; missing or censored outcomes and\nrepeated visits require a separately declared observation mechanism. The\ninferential target is the assignment-invariance hypothesis for these $N$\nunits, not an estimand for a wider population.\n\nInference in this page holds $\\mathcal I$ and $\\theta$ fixed and averages\nover the declared law $p$; it does not condition on the realized assignment.\nThe status of the quantities is explicit: $\\theta$ and all\n$Y_i(z)$ are fixed, $Z$ is random, $(Z,Y_\\theta(Z))$ is observed, and\npotential responses under unassigned vectors are fixed but latent. The\nassignment set, weights, and implemented randomization protocol are\ndesign-controlled and should be checked against protocol records. Assignment\nlogs and response records are empirically assessable, but those checks do not\nprove that the declared law generated the data. Potential outcomes under\nunassigned vectors are fixed and latent. Their values are\n**substantively untestable** from the observed-data law; the\nassignment-invariance claim is a hypothesis tested by the theorem, not an\nempirical fact supplied by the design. These classifications do not make\nthe design or scientific model empirically true.\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-covariance-bilinearity-and-symmetry",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$ and $(Y_j)_{j\\in J}$ be finite families of square-integrable real random variables on one common probability space. Then covariance is symmetric, and for real scalars $a_i,b_j$,\n$$\\operatorname{Cov}\\!\\left(\\sum_{i\\in I}a_iX_i,\\sum_{j\\in J}b_jY_j\\right)=\\sum_{i\\in I}\\sum_{j\\in J}a_ib_j\\operatorname{Cov}(X_i,Y_j).$$\nThis includes empty sums and constant summands. On a finite probability space all real random variables are square-integrable, so the original finite-space case is included with the same centered-product covariance.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.3"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Embed the pair's design law in the finite binary assignment model on $\\{0,1\\}^2$ by giving weight $1/2$ to $(1,0)$ and $(0,1)$ and weight zero to $(0,0)$ and $(1,1)$. Zero-probability assignments are allowed by [F1]. The positive-weight assignments give the displayed $D_1,D_2$, with average $(\\delta_1+\\delta_2)/2$ and difference $(\\delta_1-\\delta_2)+2(Y_1(0)-Y_2(0))$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Define $D$ by the treated-minus-control formula on the two positive-weight assignments and, for example, by zero on the two zero-weight assignments. This is a function on the full-power-set data space [F7], hence a measurable statistic and estimator of $\\tau$ by [F6]; its arbitrary zero-weight values do not affect design moments.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "By [F4], $\\mathbb E_d[D]=(D_1+D_2)/2=(\\delta_1+\\delta_2)/2=\\tau$, so the contrast estimates the pair's finite-population average effect without bias.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-3",
          "claim": "Let $X$ indicate that unit $1$ is assigned treatment $1$, so $D=D_2+(D_1-D_2)X$. Since the assignment space is finite, the variables are square-integrable. By [F5] and [F8], covariance with a constant is zero and $\\operatorname{Var}_d(D)=(D_1-D_2)^2\\operatorname{Var}_d(X)$.",
          "step": "2.3",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "F8",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The two equally weighted assignments give $\\mathbb E_d[X]=1/2$ by [F4], hence $\\operatorname{Var}_d(X)=1/4$ by [F5]. Therefore $\\operatorname{Var}_d(D)=(D_1-D_2)^2/4$, which is the formula in the Statement.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "step 2.3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: The claim is for exactly two units and two treatments; the finite assignment space has two positive-weight allocations."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: Potential outcomes may include zero values; the variance remains the squared difference between the two possible observed contrasts divided by four."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: If D_1=D_2 the observed contrast is constant and its variance is zero; equal unit-level treatment effects alone need not make D_1=D_2."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: The observed contrast is degenerate exactly when D_1=D_2, including cases with unequal baselines and effects that cancel in the displayed contrast difference."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No interval or endpoint parameter is defined."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: The two assignments are explicitly enumerated; finite expectation and covariance algebra use no choice axiom."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No equivalence is asserted."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No equivalence is asserted."
        }
      ],
      "finite_checks": [],
      "finite_smoke": []
    },
    "prop-rcbd-treatment-block-interaction-confounded-with-residual": {
      "kind": "proposition",
      "risk": "medium",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-rcbd-nested-contrast-model-and-projection",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $t,b\\ge2$, $1\\le q\\le t-1$, and let $C\\in\\mathbb R^{q\\times t}$ have\nrank $q$ and satisfy $C\\mathbf 1_t=0$. Give $V=\\mathbb R^{t\\times b}$ the\nFrobenius inner product $\\langle u,v\\rangle=\\sum_{i,j}u_{ij}v_{ij}$. Write\n$\\operatorname{row}(C)=\\operatorname{im}(C^T)$ for the span of the rows of $C$,\nand define the full additive mean space\n$$W_U=\\{(\\mu+\\tau_i+\\beta_j)_{ij}:\\mu\\in\\mathbb R,\\ \\sum_i\\tau_i=0,\\ \\sum_j\\beta_j=0\\}$$\nand the reduced space $W_R(C)$ by the same formula with the extra restriction\n$C\\tau=0$. For an array $y$, write\n$\\bar y_{i\\cdot}=b^{-1}\\sum_jy_{ij}$,\n$\\bar y_{\\cdot j}=t^{-1}\\sum_iy_{ij}$, and\n$\\bar y_{\\cdot\\cdot}=(tb)^{-1}\\sum_{i,j}y_{ij}$. Let\n$RSS_U=\\|y-P_{W_U}y\\|^2$ and $RSS_R=\\|y-P_{W_R(C)}y\\|^2$, where $P_W$ is\nthe orthogonal projection onto $W$ ([[def-orthogonal-projection]]). Then\n$$\\dim W_U=t+b-1,\\qquad \\dim W_R(C)=t+b-1-q,$$\n$$W_U^\\perp=E:=\\{r\\in\\mathbb R^{t\\times b}:\\sum_jr_{ij}=0\\text{ for every }i,\\ \\sum_ir_{ij}=0\\text{ for every }j\\},\\qquad \\dim E=(t-1)(b-1),$$\n$$RSS_U=\\sum_{i,j}(y_{ij}-\\bar y_{i\\cdot}-\\bar y_{\\cdot j}+\\bar y_{\\cdot\\cdot})^2,$$\nand\n$$RSS_R-RSS_U=b(C\\bar y)^T(CC^T)^{-1}(C\\bar y),$$\nwhere $\\bar y=(\\bar y_{1\\cdot},\\ldots,\\bar y_{t\\cdot})^T$.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-randomized-complete-block-design",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix integers $t\\ge2$ and $b\\ge1$. There are $b$ blocks, each containing the\nsame $t$ fixed experimental units, indexed within block $j$ by\n$i\\in\\{1,\\ldots,t\\}$. The $t$ treatments form a fixed set $\\mathcal T$ with\n$|\\mathcal T|=t$. A **complete block** is a block in which each treatment is\nassigned to exactly one unit. The blocks and their units are fixed before\nrandomization.\n\nLet $S_t$ be the set of permutations from the unit labels to $\\mathcal T$.\nThe **randomized complete-block design (RCBD)** uses assignment space\n$\\mathcal Z=(S_t)^b$ and the uniform probability law on this finite nonempty\nset. Thus each $z=(\\pi_1,\\ldots,\\pi_b)$ has probability $(t!)^{-b}$; the\nwithin-block treatment permutations are independent and individually uniform.\nThis is a finite randomized-treatment assignment model\n([[def-finite-randomized-treatment-assignment-model]]) whose units are the\n$tb$ cells and whose assignment is the only randomness supplied by the\ndesign. The design specifies no response model, Gaussian errors, additivity,\nor absence of interference. A pair is the special case $t=2,b=1$.\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Write the cell array as $Y=m+\\gamma+\\epsilon$, where $m\\in W_U$ is additive and $\\gamma\\in E$. By [F1], the residual from projection onto $W_U$ is $(I-P_{W_U})Y=\\gamma+(I-P_{W_U})\\epsilon$, and the latter error projection lies in $E=W_U^\\perp$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For any $h\\in E$, the identity $\\gamma+(I-P_{W_U})\\epsilon=(\\gamma+h)+((I-P_{W_U})\\epsilon-h)$ gives two decompositions of the same observed residual into vectors in $E$. Thus the cell data alone do not distinguish the unrestricted interaction component from the projected error component.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The additive space has dimension $t+b-1$ and $E$ has dimension $(t-1)(b-1)$, so their orthogonal direct sum has dimension $tb$, the full cell-space dimension. Therefore the model with a free interaction uses all cell-space directions and leaves zero residual degrees of freedom for a separate pure-error estimate.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The statement assumes t,b≥2 and one real observation in every treatment-by-block cell."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: The interaction may be zero; the proposition concerns the residual subspace and remains true."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: For the minimal t=b=2 design the residual subspace has dimension one, so the nonunique decomposition is already present."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The claim concerns unrestricted double-centered interactions; restricted interaction models can leave residual directions and are outside this statement."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No interval or endpoint parameter is defined."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: Only finite-dimensional projections and algebra are used; no choice axiom is used."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No equivalence is asserted."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No equivalence is asserted."
        }
      ],
      "finite_checks": [],
      "finite_smoke": []
    },
    "thm-rcbd-treatment-contrast-f-law": {
      "kind": "theorem",
      "risk": "high",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> ([[def-choice-function]]).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-gaussian-linear-model-qr-and-exact-t-f-pivots",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Fix a deterministic matrix $X\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times p}$ with $n>p\\ge1$ whose $p$ columns are linearly independent. Use the experiment on $(\\mathbb R^n,\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R^n))$ indexed by $(\\beta,\\sigma^2)\\in\\mathbb R^p\\times(0,\\infty)$, with observation $Y=X\\beta+\\varepsilon$ and independent errors $\\varepsilon_i\\sim N(0,\\sigma^2)$. Define the least-squares statistic and residual sum of squares by\n$$\\widehat\\beta=(X^TX)^{-1}X^TY,\\qquad P_X=X(X^TX)^{-1}X^T,\\qquad RSS_X=\\|(I_n-P_X)Y\\|^2,\\qquad S^2=\\frac{RSS_X}{n-p},\\qquad S=\\sqrt{S^2}.$$\nFor every nonzero $c\\in\\mathbb R^p$,\n$$\\widetilde T_c:=\\begin{cases} \\dfrac{c^T\\widehat\\beta-c^T\\beta}{S\\sqrt{c^T(X^TX)^{-1}c}},&S>0,\\\\ 0,&S=0 \\end{cases} \\sim t_{n-p},\\qquad \\frac{RSS_X}{\\sigma^2}\\sim\\chi_{n-p}^2,$$\nand $\\widehat\\beta$ is independent of $RSS_X$, with $S>0$ almost surely.\n\nFor nested deterministic full and reduced design spaces $W_R\\subset W_U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ of dimensions $p-q$ and $p$, where $q$ is an integer and $1\\le q\\le p<n$, let $P_R,P_U$ be the orthogonal projections onto these spaces and define $RSS_R=\\|(I-P_R)Y\\|^2$ and $RSS_U=\\|(I-P_U)Y\\|^2$. Under the null hypothesis that the mean vector belongs to $W_R$,\n$$\\widetilde F:=\\begin{cases} \\dfrac{(RSS_R-RSS_U)/q}{RSS_U/(n-p)},&RSS_U>0,\\\\ 0,&RSS_U=0 \\end{cases} \\sim F_{q,n-p}.$$\nThe numerator and denominator chi-square contributions are independent. All t and F denominator degrees of freedom in the statement are positive.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-rcbd-nested-contrast-model-and-projection",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $t,b\\ge2$, $1\\le q\\le t-1$, and let $C\\in\\mathbb R^{q\\times t}$ have\nrank $q$ and satisfy $C\\mathbf 1_t=0$. Give $V=\\mathbb R^{t\\times b}$ the\nFrobenius inner product $\\langle u,v\\rangle=\\sum_{i,j}u_{ij}v_{ij}$. Write\n$\\operatorname{row}(C)=\\operatorname{im}(C^T)$ for the span of the rows of $C$,\nand define the full additive mean space\n$$W_U=\\{(\\mu+\\tau_i+\\beta_j)_{ij}:\\mu\\in\\mathbb R,\\ \\sum_i\\tau_i=0,\\ \\sum_j\\beta_j=0\\}$$\nand the reduced space $W_R(C)$ by the same formula with the extra restriction\n$C\\tau=0$. For an array $y$, write\n$\\bar y_{i\\cdot}=b^{-1}\\sum_jy_{ij}$,\n$\\bar y_{\\cdot j}=t^{-1}\\sum_iy_{ij}$, and\n$\\bar y_{\\cdot\\cdot}=(tb)^{-1}\\sum_{i,j}y_{ij}$. Let\n$RSS_U=\\|y-P_{W_U}y\\|^2$ and $RSS_R=\\|y-P_{W_R(C)}y\\|^2$, where $P_W$ is\nthe orthogonal projection onto $W$ ([[def-orthogonal-projection]]). Then\n$$\\dim W_U=t+b-1,\\qquad \\dim W_R(C)=t+b-1-q,$$\n$$W_U^\\perp=E:=\\{r\\in\\mathbb R^{t\\times b}:\\sum_jr_{ij}=0\\text{ for every }i,\\ \\sum_ir_{ij}=0\\text{ for every }j\\},\\qquad \\dim E=(t-1)(b-1),$$\n$$RSS_U=\\sum_{i,j}(y_{ij}-\\bar y_{i\\cdot}-\\bar y_{\\cdot j}+\\bar y_{\\cdot\\cdot})^2,$$\nand\n$$RSS_R-RSS_U=b(C\\bar y)^T(CC^T)^{-1}(C\\bar y),$$\nwhere $\\bar y=(\\bar y_{1\\cdot},\\ldots,\\bar y_{t\\cdot})^T$.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-student-t-and-f-laws",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. For a positive integer $k$, take the product probability law of an independent standard normal $G$ and an independent $Q_k\\sim\\chi_k^2$. The **Student t law with $k$ degrees of freedom**, denoted $t_k$, is the law of\n$$T_k=\\begin{cases} \\displaystyle\\frac{G}{\\sqrt{Q_k/k}},&Q_k>0,\\\\ 0,&Q_k\\le0. \\end{cases}$$\nFor positive integers $r,s$, take independent $Q_r\\sim\\chi_r^2$ and $Q_s\\sim\\chi_s^2$. The **Fisher F law with degrees $(r,s)$**, denoted $F_{r,s}$, is the law of\n$$F_{r,s}=\\begin{cases} \\displaystyle\\frac{Q_r/r}{Q_s/s},&Q_s>0,\\\\ 0,&Q_s\\le0. \\end{cases}$$\nThe displayed piecewise maps are defined on all of $\\mathbb R^2$ and are Borel measurable: each quotient is continuous on its Borel positive-denominator region, and the value on the complementary Borel nonpositive-denominator region is constant. The positive-degree chi-square lemma proves that the denominators are positive almost surely, so these versions agree almost surely with the usual quotients. Zero degrees are excluded from both definitions.\n",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-statistic-and-estimator",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathsf E=((\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A),\\Theta,(P_\\theta)_{\\theta\\in\\Theta})$\nbe an experiment ([[def-statistical-experiment]]). A **statistic** with target\nmeasurable space $(\\mathcal Y,\\mathcal B)$ is a measurable map\n$T:(\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A)\\to(\\mathcal Y,\\mathcal B)$, where measurability\nmeans that $T^{-1}(B)\\in\\mathcal A$ for every $B\\in\\mathcal B$\n([[def-measurable-function-between-measurable-spaces]]). If\n$\\psi:\\Theta\\to\\mathcal Y$ is an estimand ([[def-estimand-on-a-statistical-model]])\nand its target set $\\mathcal Y$ is equipped with $\\mathcal B$, a statistic\n$\\widehat\\psi$ with this same target space is an **estimator of $\\psi$**.\nThe value $\\widehat\\psi(x)$ is the estimate at observed data $x$. Giving\n$\\mathcal Y$ a measurable structure is an additional choice; this definition\ndoes not assert that $\\psi$ is measurable as a function of the parameter.\n",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-finite-randomized-treatment-assignment-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $N\\ge1$, and let $\\mathcal I=\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$ index the fixed\nexperimental units at the actual treatment-assignment level. Let\n$\\mathcal Z$ be a nonempty finite set of allowed assignments. If treatment\nis assigned to clusters, each cluster is one indexed unit; no separate\nprobability-sampling unit is present. The index $i$ identifies that unit,\nand no visit or repeated-measurement index is modeled here.\n\nA known assignment law is a function $p:\\mathcal Z\\to[0,1]$ satisfying\n$\\sum_{z\\in\\mathcal Z}p(z)=1$, so $(\\mathcal Z,p)$ is a finite probability\nspace ([[def-finite-probability-space-and-event]]); assignments of probability\nzero may remain in $\\mathcal Z$. A **potential-outcome schedule** is a function\n$\\theta:\\mathcal I\\times\\mathcal Z\\to\\mathbb R$, with\n$Y_i(z):=\\theta(i,z)$. The schedule and the units are fixed; the assignment\n$Z$ is the only randomness supplied by the design, and the observed response\nvector is $Y_\\theta(Z)=(Y_i(Z))_{i\\in\\mathcal I}$. The schedule is defined\nfor every allowed assignment, including zero-probability assignments, and\nmay depend on the full assignment vector, so no no-interference assumption is\nimplicit. The scientific statistical model contract\n[[def-scientific-statistical-model-contract]] requires the observation space,\nunits, quantity status, observation mechanism, support, conditioning\nconvention, and adequacy assumptions to be recorded; this model specifies\nthose fields as follows.\n\nTo express this as a statistical experiment, take the data space\n$\\mathcal X=\\mathcal Z\\times\\mathbb R^{\\mathcal I}$ with its full power-set\nsigma-algebra, which is a sigma-algebra by [[def-sigma-algebra]] and makes\n$\\mathcal X$ a measurable space ([[def-measurable-space]]), and parameter\nset $\\Theta=\\mathbb R^{\\mathcal I\\times\\mathcal Z}$. For each $\\theta\\in\\Theta$\nlet $P_\\theta$ be the law\n([[def-law-or-distribution-of-a-random-element]]) of the deterministic\nobservation map $O_\\theta(z)=(z,Y_\\theta(z))$ from $(\\mathcal Z,p)$. The map\nis measurable because its domain has the full power set, in the preimage\nsense of [[def-measurable-function-between-measurable-spaces]], and its law\nis a probability measure by\n[[lem-law-of-a-random-element-is-a-probability-measure]]. Thus\n$((\\mathcal X,2^{\\mathcal X}),\\Theta,(P_\\theta)_{\\theta\\in\\Theta})$ is an\nexperiment in the sense of [[def-statistical-experiment]]. The assignment\nlaw asserts no response-error model, Gaussian law, superpopulation\nrandomization, or sampling representativeness beyond the stated finite\nassignment mechanism. Its finite full-measure support set is\n$$S_\\theta:=\\{(z,Y_\\theta(z)):z\\in\\mathcal Z,\\ p(z)>0\\},$$\nsince $P_\\theta(S_\\theta)=\\sum_{z:p(z)>0}p(z)=1$. The record contains the\nassigned vector and every unit's response; missing or censored outcomes and\nrepeated visits require a separately declared observation mechanism. The\ninferential target is the assignment-invariance hypothesis for these $N$\nunits, not an estimand for a wider population.\n\nInference in this page holds $\\mathcal I$ and $\\theta$ fixed and averages\nover the declared law $p$; it does not condition on the realized assignment.\nThe status of the quantities is explicit: $\\theta$ and all\n$Y_i(z)$ are fixed, $Z$ is random, $(Z,Y_\\theta(Z))$ is observed, and\npotential responses under unassigned vectors are fixed but latent. The\nassignment set, weights, and implemented randomization protocol are\ndesign-controlled and should be checked against protocol records. Assignment\nlogs and response records are empirically assessable, but those checks do not\nprove that the declared law generated the data. Potential outcomes under\nunassigned vectors are fixed and latent. Their values are\n**substantively untestable** from the observed-data law; the\nassignment-invariance claim is a hypothesis tested by the theorem, not an\nempirical fact supplied by the design. These classifications do not make\nthe design or scientific model empirically true.\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "By [F2], $W_R(C)\\subset W_U$, $p=\\dim W_U=t+b-1$, $q=\\dim W_U-\\dim W_R(C)$, and $n=tb$. Since $t,b\\ge2$, $n-p=tb-t-b+1=(t-1)(b-1)=\\nu>0$, and $q\\le t-1\\le p$. Under $C\\tau=0$, the mean array $(\\mu+\\tau_i+\\beta_j)_{ij}$ belongs to $W_R(C)$. Thus the nested-space, positive-degree, and null-mean hypotheses of [F1] hold.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Applying [F1] gives the piecewise nested-model statistic $\\{(RSS_R-RSS_U)/q\\}/\\{RSS_U/\\nu\\}$ the law $F_{q,\\nu}$, with its value set to zero when $RSS_U=0$. The finite assignment law [F5] supplies no normal-error assumption; Gaussianity here is separately imposed in the Given. AC is used exactly through [A1] and [F1].",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "By [F2], the numerator reduction is $b(C\\bar Y)^T(CC^T)^{-1}(C\\bar Y)$, so the statistic in the Statement is the same piecewise ratio. Its numerator and residual sum of squares are continuous functions of the data, and the ratio with the stated zero-denominator branch is Borel measurable; hence it is a statistic by [F4].",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Since $q,\\nu>0$, [F3] defines the named central law and step 3.1 is the piecewise nested-model ratio from [F1]. It therefore has law $F_{q,\\nu}$, as claimed.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: The hypotheses require t,b≥2 and 1≤q≤t−1, so p<n and both F degrees are positive."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: σ²=0 is excluded; σ²>0 is required by the nested Gaussian theorem."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: The smallest t=b=2,q=1 case has p=3,n=4 and ν=1, so the denominator degrees remain positive."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: The null Cτ=0 is included; alternatives are not assigned a distributional claim here."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No interval or endpoint parameter is defined."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: AC is explicitly assumed and placed in deps because the Gaussian supplier uses it in the standard-normal construction and orthogonal invariance."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No equivalence is asserted."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No equivalence is asserted."
        }
      ],
      "finite_checks": [],
      "finite_smoke": []
    },
    "ex-missing-treatment-is-not-a-complete-block": {
      "kind": "example",
      "risk": "low",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-randomized-complete-block-design",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix integers $t\\ge2$ and $b\\ge1$. There are $b$ blocks, each containing the\nsame $t$ fixed experimental units, indexed within block $j$ by\n$i\\in\\{1,\\ldots,t\\}$. The $t$ treatments form a fixed set $\\mathcal T$ with\n$|\\mathcal T|=t$. A **complete block** is a block in which each treatment is\nassigned to exactly one unit. The blocks and their units are fixed before\nrandomization.\n\nLet $S_t$ be the set of permutations from the unit labels to $\\mathcal T$.\nThe **randomized complete-block design (RCBD)** uses assignment space\n$\\mathcal Z=(S_t)^b$ and the uniform probability law on this finite nonempty\nset. Thus each $z=(\\pi_1,\\ldots,\\pi_b)$ has probability $(t!)^{-b}$; the\nwithin-block treatment permutations are independent and individually uniform.\nThis is a finite randomized-treatment assignment model\n([[def-finite-randomized-treatment-assignment-model]]) whose units are the\n$tb$ cells and whose assignment is the only randomness supplied by the\ndesign. The design specifies no response model, Gaussian errors, additivity,\nor absence of interference. A pair is the special case $t=2,b=1$.\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The image of this block's assignment is $\\{A,C\\}$, which omits $B$, so the map is not onto $\\{A,B,C\\}$ and is not a permutation.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Every assignment in the RCBD sample space is a permutation, while this assignment is not; therefore this block is incomplete and outside that sample space.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The example specifies one block and three treatment labels."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: No probability calculation is asserted; the issue is membership in the assignment space."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: A block receiving all three treatments once would be complete; omitting one treatment fails the condition."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: The result is independent of response values and outcomes."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No interval or endpoint parameter is defined."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: The finite assignment space is explicitly characterized by permutations."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No equivalence is asserted."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No equivalence is asserted."
        }
      ],
      "finite_checks": [],
      "finite_smoke": []
    },
    "ex-latin-square-assignment-is-restricted": {
      "kind": "example",
      "risk": "low",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-randomized-complete-block-design",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix integers $t\\ge2$ and $b\\ge1$. There are $b$ blocks, each containing the\nsame $t$ fixed experimental units, indexed within block $j$ by\n$i\\in\\{1,\\ldots,t\\}$. The $t$ treatments form a fixed set $\\mathcal T$ with\n$|\\mathcal T|=t$. A **complete block** is a block in which each treatment is\nassigned to exactly one unit. The blocks and their units are fixed before\nrandomization.\n\nLet $S_t$ be the set of permutations from the unit labels to $\\mathcal T$.\nThe **randomized complete-block design (RCBD)** uses assignment space\n$\\mathcal Z=(S_t)^b$ and the uniform probability law on this finite nonempty\nset. Thus each $z=(\\pi_1,\\ldots,\\pi_b)$ has probability $(t!)^{-b}$; the\nwithin-block treatment permutations are independent and individually uniform.\nThis is a finite randomized-treatment assignment model\n([[def-finite-randomized-treatment-assignment-model]]) whose units are the\n$tb$ cells and whose assignment is the only randomness supplied by the\ndesign. The design specifies no response model, Gaussian errors, additivity,\nor absence of interference. A pair is the special case $t=2,b=1$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-randomized-latin-square-design",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix $t\\ge1$ and put $R_t=\\{0,1,\\ldots,t-1\\}$, with addition interpreted\nmodulo $t$. A **Latin-square allocation of order $t$** is a function\n$L:R_t\\times R_t\\to R_t$ such that for\neach fixed row the map from columns to treatments is a bijection, and for\neach fixed column the map from rows to treatments is a bijection. Thus each\ntreatment occurs exactly once in every row and every column. Let\n$\\mathcal L_t$ be the finite set of these allocations and assign the uniform\nlaw on $\\mathcal L_t$. This law is well-defined: $L_0(r,c)=r+c\\pmod t$ is a\nLatin square, since adding a fixed residue modulo $t$ permutes the residues; hence\n$\\mathcal L_t$ is nonempty. This specifies one exact\nrestricted randomization law. It randomizes over treatment-by-row-by-column\nassignments and, for $t\\ge2$, is distinct from the product of independent\nwithin-block permutations in an RCBD ([[def-randomized-complete-block-design]]).",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "In the cyclic array, each row and each column contains $A,B,C$ once, so it belongs to the Latin-square sample space.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "In the repeated-row array, each row contains $A,B,C$ once, so it is an RCBD assignment; each column repeats a single treatment, so it is not a Latin square.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "There are $3!=6$ permutations per RCBD block and the blocks are independently uniform, so the particular repeated-row assignment has probability $6^{-3}=1/216$. It has probability zero under a law supported only on Latin squares.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The example fixes three treatment labels and three blocks."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: The repeated-row assignment has zero weight under the Latin-square law because it is not in its support."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: The specified cyclic array is an admissible Latin square and therefore has positive uniform mass."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: This comparison is about assignment support and does not depend on observed responses."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No interval or endpoint parameter is defined."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Both sample spaces are finite; the Latin-square set is nonempty by the cyclic construction in its definition."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No equivalence is asserted."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No equivalence is asserted."
        }
      ],
      "finite_checks": [],
      "finite_smoke": []
    },
    "ex-paired-randomization-contrast-with-heterogeneous-effects": {
      "kind": "example",
      "risk": "low",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-randomized-complete-block-design",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix integers $t\\ge2$ and $b\\ge1$. There are $b$ blocks, each containing the\nsame $t$ fixed experimental units, indexed within block $j$ by\n$i\\in\\{1,\\ldots,t\\}$. The $t$ treatments form a fixed set $\\mathcal T$ with\n$|\\mathcal T|=t$. A **complete block** is a block in which each treatment is\nassigned to exactly one unit. The blocks and their units are fixed before\nrandomization.\n\nLet $S_t$ be the set of permutations from the unit labels to $\\mathcal T$.\nThe **randomized complete-block design (RCBD)** uses assignment space\n$\\mathcal Z=(S_t)^b$ and the uniform probability law on this finite nonempty\nset. Thus each $z=(\\pi_1,\\ldots,\\pi_b)$ has probability $(t!)^{-b}$; the\nwithin-block treatment permutations are independent and individually uniform.\nThis is a finite randomized-treatment assignment model\n([[def-finite-randomized-treatment-assignment-model]]) whose units are the\n$tb$ cells and whose assignment is the only randomness supplied by the\ndesign. The design specifies no response model, Gaussian errors, additivity,\nor absence of interference. A pair is the special case $t=2,b=1$.\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-expectation-on-a-finite-probability-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **expectation** of a real random variable $X$ on a finite probability space $(\\Omega,w)$ is\n$$\\mathbb E[X]:=\\sum_{\\omega\\in\\Omega}X(\\omega)w(\\omega).$$\nFor a real constant $c$, the notation $c$ also denotes the constant random variable $\\omega\\mapsto c$.\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-moments-variance-and-covariance",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a real random variable on a probability space.\n\n- For $p>0$, the **$p$th absolute moment** of $X$ exists when $|X|^p$ is\n  integrable, and is then\n  $$\\mathbb E[|X|^p].$$\n- If $X$ is integrable, its **mean** is $\\mathbb E[X]$.\n- If $X$ is square-integrable, its **variance** is\n  $$\\operatorname{Var}(X):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])^2].$$\n\nIf $X$ and $Y$ are square-integrable real random variables on the same\nprobability space, their **covariance** is\n$$\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])(Y-\\mathbb E[Y])].$$\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "If unit $1$ receives treatment, the contrast is $Y_1(1)-Y_2(0)=4$; if unit $2$ receives treatment, it is $Y_2(1)-Y_1(0)=2$. They are equally likely by [F1].",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "By [F2] the contrast mean is $(4+2)/2=3$, the pair average effect. By [F3] its variance is $\\{(4-3)^2+(2-3)^2\\}/2=1$. Thus the example has unequal unit effects and directly computes the observed contrast moments.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The example has a two-unit pair and two assignments."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: The listed effects are positive; the general theorem also covers zero effects."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: The effects are unequal, so the design variance is positive."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "If the effects were equal, the theorem would give zero variance; this example is outside that boundary."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No interval or endpoint parameter is defined."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: The two assignments are directly enumerated."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No equivalence is asserted."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No equivalence is asserted."
        }
      ],
      "finite_checks": [],
      "finite_smoke": [
        {
          "check": "symmetric-rademacher-covariance",
          "asserts": "The centered paired-contrast values are $+1$ and $-1$ with equal weights, so their variance is $1$."
        }
      ]
    },
    "ex-rcbd-interaction-shares-the-residual-subspace": {
      "kind": "example",
      "risk": "low",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "prop-rcbd-treatment-block-interaction-confounded-with-residual",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $t,b\\ge2$ and observe one real response $Y_{ij}$ for each treatment $i$\nand block $j$. In the additive mean model, the residual subspace consists of\nthe double-centered arrays\n$$E=\\{\\gamma\\in\\mathbb R^{t\\times b}:\\sum_j\\gamma_{ij}=0\\ \\text{for every }i,\\ \\sum_i\\gamma_{ij}=0\\ \\text{for every }j\\},$$\nwhich has dimension $(t-1)(b-1)$ ([[lem-rcbd-nested-contrast-model-and-projection]]).\nIf a treatment-by-block interaction $\\gamma\\in E$ is included in the mean,\nthen the additive-fit residual is $\\gamma+P_E\\epsilon$, where $P_E\\epsilon$\nis the projection of the cell error onto $E$. For every $h\\in E$ this same\nresidual equals $(\\gamma+h)+(P_E\\epsilon-h)$. Hence one observation per cell\ndoes not separately decompose the realized residual into unrestricted\ninteraction and projected error, and fitting the unrestricted interaction\nleaves zero pure-error residual degrees of freedom. This is an algebraic\ndesign limitation; it does not assert nonidentifiability under every\nadditional stochastic model.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Each row and each column of $R$ sums to zero, so $R$ is double-centered and belongs to that residual subspace.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Both pairs $(\\gamma,e)=(R,0)$ and $(\\gamma,e)=(0,R)$ consist of vectors in the residual subspace and sum to $R$; hence they yield the identical observed residual with different interaction/error allocations.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The example uses one observation in each of four treatment-by-block cells."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: The zero array is also in the residual subspace; the displayed nonzero array witnesses the ambiguity."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The two-by-two design has one residual direction, and the example uses that direction."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: The ambiguity concerns the realized additive residual, not a probability law for the error."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No interval or endpoint parameter is defined."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The example is a direct finite array calculation."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No equivalence is asserted."
        },
